Bob claims that the following program always correctly searches for target in a non-decreasing array: it returns any matching index if target is present, and −1 otherwise. Write a program that reads no input: if the algorithm is correct, output CORRECT; otherwise output any valid counterexample array and target. This site follows the complete Java version of the original OCR053; the program to refute is:

```java
int sorted_search(int[] elements, int target) {
    if (elements == null || elements.length <= 0) return -1;
    int left = 0, right = elements.length - 1;
    while (left < right) {
        int middle = (left + right + 1) / 2;
        if (elements[middle] > target) right = middle - 1;
        else left = middle + 1;
    }
    if (elements[right] == target) return right;
    return -1;
}
```

The judge accepts any valid witness that makes this algorithm produce a wrong result; it does not have to match the public example you see.
